<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Gyeongsang Province</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gyeongsangbuk-do</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North Gyeongsang is a province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, with its government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a> since 2016. Its lands include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, the old capital of Silla, the Gaya centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryeong">Goryeong</a> and the cities of Pohang, Gumi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangju">Sangju</a>. It was created in 1896, when the old Gyeongsang province was divided into northern and southern halves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-gyeongsang-province.jpg" alt="North Gyeongsang Province" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Contax3 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%A3%BC%EC%82%B0%EC%A7%80-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That text places it at Hamchang, near modern Sangju in <b>North Gyeongsang</b>, though some scholars doubt the identification.</blockquote><a href="/state/goryeong-gaya">Goryeong Gaya</a></li></ul></section>
